<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/expert/religion-in-the-modern-world">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The modern world was supposed to make religion disappear. It didn't — it <strong>reorganised
    it</strong>: secular Europe, religious America, resurgent public religion, new movements. And
    one of the best empirical windows on what actually happened is the unit this lesson crowns:
    <em>social action</em>. The three Abrahamic traditions' organised care for others — feeding,
    healing, resettling, planting — is where religion meets the state, the market and global
    crisis on measurable ground. This chapter walks Christianity, Islam and Judaism through
    their social action: the teachings, the environmental theologies, the named organisations —
    and then asks what all that activity proves about the secularisation thesis.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the secularisation thesis, fundamentalism, new movements, and public religion" widget="equationStep" config={modernCfg} />

  <h2>Christianity: concern for others as doctrine</h2>
  <p>
    Christian social action rests on <em>agape</em> — self-giving love modelled on God's — and
    two anchor texts: the parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25–37), which makes the needy
    stranger one's neighbour across ethnic and religious lines, and the parable of the sheep
    and goats (Matthew 25:31–46), where the King judges nations by how they treated "the least
    of these" — feeding, clothing, welcoming, visiting. The theology matters for practice:
    because salvation is by grace rather than earned, Christian charity is framed as response,
    not currency — which is why it historically organised into institutions (hospitals, alms,
    orders) rather than spiritual bookkeeping. The incarnational logic adds a doctrine of
    dignity: God taking human flesh makes bodily need — hunger, illness, displacement — a
    theological matter, not merely a social one.
  </p>

  <h2>Christian stewardship of the environment</h2>
  <p>
    Two creation texts frame the debate: Genesis 1:28 ("have dominion") was long read as
    licence; the stewardship reading pairs it with Genesis 2:15 — humanity placed in the garden
    "to till and keep it" — as trusteeship, not ownership. The modern theology is explicit:
    Pope Francis's encyclical <em>Laudato Si'</em> (2015) argued creation care, poverty and
    technology critique are one subject ("the cry of the earth and the cry of the poor"); the
    Church of England set a net-zero emissions target for 2030 (General Synod, 2020); Eco
    Church schemes, fossil-divestment campaigns and interfast climate coalitions institutionalised
    it. The underlying argument is practical as much as doctrinal: a body of 2.4 billion
    adherents, landholdings, schools and pulpits is infrastructure — and stewardship is the
    doctrine that deploys it.
  </p>

  <h2>Christian organisations that relieve poverty</h2>
  <p>
    The institutional record is deep and specific: the Salvation Army (founded 1865 by William
    Booth) grew from East London mission to a global social-service network; Christian Aid
    (1945, British and Irish churches) and CAFOD (1962, Catholic) turned denominational
    fund-raising into professional development agencies; Tearfund (1968) and World Vision
    (1950) complete the standard row. Their models matter analytically: emergency relief,
    long-term development, and <em>campaigning</em> (debt relief — the Jubilee 2000 coalition
    that cancelled billions of poor-country debt — being the showcase of faith-based advocacy),
    usually with local church partners who reach places states and NGOs cannot. Faith-based
    agencies deliver a substantial share of global humanitarian response — commonly estimated
    at a third or more — which is exactly why the sector's ethics (non-proselytising aid
    standards) are codified.
  </p>

  <h2>Islam: the ummah as a single community</h2>
  <p>
    The Quran names the community one: "this ummah of yours is one ummah, and I am your Lord"
    (21:92) — a transnational brotherhood of believers above tribe, ethnicity and state. Hajj
    makes it visible annually (roughly 1.8 million pilgrims in 2024): the largest recurring
    human gathering, identical dress, one direction of prayer. The ummah's social- action logic
    is redistribution: zakat moves wealth from richer to poorer Muslims wherever they are, and
    Ramadan's solidarity disciplines (fasting as shared experience of need) feed the giving
    season. Modernity complicated the picture without dissolving it: nation-states partitioned
    the ummah legally, while media and migration re-wove it socially — Muslim humanitarian
    response to disasters anywhere in the Muslim world is the ummah concept operating as
    infrastructure, not sentiment.
  </p>

  <h2>Shari'ah: ethics, law and the straight path</h2>
  <p>
    Shari'ah — literally "the straight path" — is drawn from the Quran and the Sunnah (the
    Prophet's practice, recorded in hadith), and developed by jurists into <em>fiqh</em>. Its
    scope is mostly personal and devotional: acts are classified from obligatory (the five
    pillars) through recommended, permitted, discouraged and forbidden; dietary law (halal),
    prayer, fasting and family ethics form the everyday core. As economics it prohibits
    <em>riba</em> (interest), producing a distinct Islamic-finance industry (cost-plus
    murabaha contracts, profit-sharing); as jurisprudence its higher objectives
    (<em>maqasid al-sharia</em>, systematised by al-Ghazali and al-Shatibi) protect life, faith,
    intellect, lineage and property. Its legal-system applications vary: state criminal law in
    some countries, family-law codes in most Muslim-majority states, and non-binding councils
    (advisory, on family matters) in diasporas including Britain — a contested but real
    institution. The study point: shari'ah is a civilisation-scale tradition of law and ethics,
    not a single code.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "shari'ah means the harsh punishments you see in headlines."
    Hudud penalties are a small, contested corner of the tradition, applied in only some states
    and disputed by the majority of Muslim jurists historically. The overwhelming bulk of
    shari'ah concerns worship, contracts, family and charity — the everyday ethics of 1.9
    billion people.
  </p>

  <h2>Islam: concern for others at home and in the community</h2>
  <p>
    Giving is structural, not voluntary in spirit: <em>zakat</em> — the third pillar — obliges
    2.5% of qualifying wealth (above the <em>nisab</em> threshold) annually for eight named
    categories of recipient (Quran 9:60), the poor first among them; <em>sadaqah</em> is
    everything voluntary above it. The Prophetic texts set the community standard: "he is not
    a believer whose stomach is filled while his neighbour goes hungry" (a hadith in Bukhari's
    collection), and the hadith that removing harm from the road counts as charity. Family
    duties (support of parents, orphans — whose care the Quran commends constantly) and the
    mosque's welfare role (imams brokering help, Ramadan food programmes) make the community
    the delivery system. Islam's concern for others is thus legalised compassion: obligation
    first, grace on top — the mirror image of the Christian order, with similar throughput.
  </p>

  <h2>Islam: khalifah of the environment</h2>
  <p>
    The environmental theology runs through <em>khalifah</em>: humanity as God's vicegerent on
    earth (Quran 2:30, 6:165) — trustees with accountability, not owners. The supporting
    concepts are precise: <em>mizan</em>, the balance built into creation (Quran 55:7–9), which
    waste (<em>israf</em>, repeatedly condemned) violates; the extended prohibition on wanton
    destruction of resources; and hadith commanding kindness to animals. Institutional form
    followed: the 2015 Islamic Declaration on Global Climate Change (Istanbul) called for
    phase-out of fossil emissions; Indonesia's senior clerical council (MUI) issued fatwas on
    protected wildlife (2014) and forest burning; eco-mosques and Islamic environment
    foundations operate from Britain to the Gulf. Khalifah re-reads "dominion" exactly as
    Christian stewardship re-reads Genesis 1:28 — the two traditions converging on trusteeship.
  </p>

  <h2>Muslim organisations that relieve poverty</h2>
  <p>
    The modern sector is professional and zakat-powered: Islamic Relief Worldwide (founded 1984
    in Birmingham by Egyptian and Somali medical students, now operating in dozens of
    countries), Muslim Aid (1985) and Muslim Hands (1993) lead a network of agencies whose
    funding base is the obligatory alms of Muslim communities — a built-in, recurring revenue
    model most NGOs envy. Their comparative advantage is operational: local partners, language
    and trust inside Muslim-majority crisis zones (Afghanistan, Yemen, Rohingya displacement),
    where they are often first in. Zakat's annual-cycle discipline and Islamic finance's
    cash-waqf endowments are extending the model into development and microfinance — the
    ummah's redistributive doctrine running through modern institutional plumbing.
  </p>

  <h2>Judaism: concern for others as justice</h2>
  <p>
    The Hebrew term is the whole argument: <em>tzedakah</em> shares a root with
    <em>tzedek</em> — justice. Giving is not kindness supererogatory but obligation: the poor
    have a right to support, and the community owes it. The tradition systematised the duty:
    Maimonides' eight levels of giving (Mishneh Torah) rank from grudging bestowal to the
    highest — strengthening a person so they no longer need help (a job, a partnership); the
    Talmudic city maintained organised funds (the daily <em>kuppah</em> and the weekly
    <em>tamchui</em> — the ancient welfare state); <em>gemilut chasadim</em> (deeds of
    lovingkindness) and <em>bikkur cholim</em> (visiting the sick) extend the duty beyond
    money. Judaism's concern for others is thus jurisprudence: rights, duties and
    institutions — which is why its philanthropic infrastructure survives every expulsion
    that scattered it.
  </p>

  <h2>Judaism: stewardship of the environment</h2>
  <p>
    The textual toolkit is old and specific. <em>Bal tashchit</em> — "you shall not destroy"
    (Deuteronomy 20:19–20, originally banning the felling of fruit trees in siege) — was
    extended by the sages into a general prohibition of wanton waste; <em>shmittah</em>, the
    sabbatical year (Leviticus 25), suspends cultivation and cancels debts, enacting the
    principle that the land belongs to God, not its farmers. <em>Tikkun olam</em> ("repair of
    the world" — a liturgical phrase modern Judaism re-purposed for social action) is the
    banner under which Jewish environmentalism now organises: the Jewish National Fund's
    century of tree-planting (literally making the land), Hazon's US programmes, and
    shmittah-inspired sustainable agriculture in Israel. As in the other traditions, the
    modern reading is a revival: the environmental virtues were already canonical, awaiting
    application.
  </p>

  <h2>Jewish organisations that relieve poverty</h2>
  <p>
    The flagship case writes itself: World Jewish Relief descends from the Central British Fund
    for German Jewry, founded in 1933 — the organisation that helped finance the Kindertransport
    rescues of Jewish children before the Second World War. The sector built around it: Jewish
    Federations across North America pool and allocate communal funds; Mazon (1985) mobilises
    the Jewish community against hunger in the US; Leket Israel operates the country's largest
    food-rescue programme (collecting surplus agricultural produce — gleaning, modernised,
    with the Torah's leave-the-edges command as its name and logic); Tzedek and World Jewish
    Relief run international development. The pattern matches the other traditions: ancient
    legal duty, modern professional delivery, distinctive scriptural branding.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "tzedakah is the Jewish word for charity — same thing as
    voluntary giving." The root means righteousness or justice: giving is owed, not optional,
    which changes everything downstream (Maimonides grades the <em>giver's attitude</em>
    because the obligation exists regardless of feeling). Charity is a virtue; tzedakah is a
    duty — the distinction the whole tradition's welfare structure rests on.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "secularisation means religion is disappearing
  everywhere." The trend line is strongly regional: institutional religion in Western
  Europe has thinned dramatically, while much of the global South is more religious than
  a century ago — and sociologists now distinguish <em>belonging</em> from <em>believing</em>
  (Grace Davie's "believing without belonging" describes Britain, not Nigeria). A thesis
  built from one continent's church attendance was never a universal law.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>What social action proves in the secularisation debate.</strong> The simple
    secularisation thesis predicted religion would retreat to the private sphere and wither.
    The social-action record is the standing counter-evidence: faith-based agencies deliver
    a third or more of global humanitarian response, religious environmentalism mobilises at
    scale, and Casanova's <em>Public Religions in the Modern World</em> (1994) formalised the
    finding — religions deprivatised rather than disappeared. The refined theses
    (existential-security theory, multiple modernities — covered in the widget's framing)
    predict exactly this pattern: religion transforms, redistributes and re-enters public
    life, most visibly wherever the state fails and faith-based networks already stand.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The comparative lesson.</strong> The three traditions encode the same care with
    different grammar: Christianity (grace, response), Islam (obligation, percentage,
    community), Judaism (justice, right of the poor). The delivery systems converge —
    professional agencies, global logistics, campaigning arms — which suggests the institutional
    form is modernity's, while the moral fuel is each tradition's own. For the exam and the
    analyst alike: compare the theology of giving (why), the mechanism (what), and the scale
    (how much), in that order.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Aid ethics: where is the line between relief and
    proselytism (the sector's codes forbid coercive conditionality — enforcement is harder)?
    Can zakat and tzedakah systems scale into formal development finance without losing their
    communal character? And does religious environmentalism move policy or only consciences —
    the empirical question the 2020s climate fight is answering in real time?
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Norris & Ingleheart, Sacred and Secular (2004); Eisenstadt, "Multiple Modernities" (2000); Pew Global Attitudes data; Casanova, Public Religions in the Modern World (1994).</p>
